<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Slavs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eastern Slavs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> are the Slavic peoples of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from whom the Russians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a> descend. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> they formed tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polans-eastern">Polianians</a>, Drevlianians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krivichs">Krivichians</a>, described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, and in the 9th and 10th centuries they were united under the Rurikid princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. They adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> after 988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-slavs.jpg" alt="East Slavs" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stegop (original image: CrazyPhunk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Slavic_Europe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
